Proof: submission `b0e7320f-1c54-5083-9cb6-32609b21623d`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Fix an integer $M$ with $M\neq 0$ and $2\le M$, and let $A_M=$ [`ModularCurve.LevelN.ring
M`](def/ModularCurve_LevelNFunctionField.html#L28) be the $\mathbb{C}$-subalgebra of functions
$\mathbb{H}\to\mathbb{C}$ generated by $j=E_4^3/\Delta$ together with the Fricke functions $f_v$
attached to the nonzero $v\in(\mathbb{Z}/M)^2$. Let $K$ be a field that is simultaneously a
$\mathbb{C}$-algebra and an $A_M$-algebra, compatibly, and is a fraction field of $A_M$. Assume:
$A_M$ is stable under precomposition with the action of every $\delta\in\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$
on $\mathbb{H}$; $\sigma$ is a group homomorphism from $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ to the
$\mathbb{C}$-algebra automorphisms of $K$ which on the image of $G\in A_M$ is given by $G\mapsto
G(\gamma^{-1}\cdot{-})$; $\ker\sigma=\Gamma(M)\vee\langle -1\rangle$; the fixed field of the range
of $\sigma$ is $\mathbb{C}\langle j\rangle$, the subfield generated over $\mathbb{C}$ by the image
$j$ of `jGen M` in $K$; and $K/\mathbb{C}\langle j\rangle$ is finite and Galois. Then there is a
place $W$ of $K$ over $\mathbb{C}$ (a proper valuation subring containing $\mathbb{C}$ whose
valuation ring is a principal ideal ring) with $\operatorname{ord}_W(j)=3$ such that every
$g\in\mathrm{Gal}(K/\mathbb{C}\langle j\rangle)$ fixing $W$ under the induced semilinear action
equals $\sigma(ST)^k$ for some $k\in\mathbb{N}$; and there is a place $W$ with
$\operatorname{ord}_W(j-1728)=2$ whose stabiliser in $\mathrm{Gal}(K/\mathbb{C}\langle j\rangle)$
consists of powers $\sigma(S)^k$, $k\in\mathbb{N}$.

This is the exact ramification behaviour of the covering $X(M)\to X(1)$ above the two elliptic
points, $e=3$ over $j=0$ with decomposition group generated by $ST$, and $e=2$ over $j=1728$ with
decomposition group generated by $S$. It is used in the arguments showing that the function fields
of $X_0(M)$ and $X_1(M)$ have no elliptic points in the relevant range, after base change to an
algebraically closed field.

